course: dictionary
lesson: 6670
title: Oath: a serious promise
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An oath is a serious promise. People often make one in front of others, or in a court, to tell the truth or to do something important.
explain: «The knight swore an oath of loyalty to his king.» The clues are loyalty and king. A knight who is loyal keeps his promises, so an oath here is a solemn promise to stay faithful.
explain: Be careful with old books. «He let out an oath when he dropped the pan» means he swore a rude word in anger, not that he made a promise. Read the clues around the word to see which meaning fits.
explain: Some phrases to know: take an oath, swear an oath, under oath, oath of loyalty. Under oath means you have promised in a court to tell the truth.
fact: An oath is a serious promise, often made in front of other people.
fact: «Under oath» means having promised in a court to tell the truth.
fact: New citizens may take an oath to their country.
fact: In old books, an oath can mean a swear word or curse said in anger.
fact: Useful phrases: take an oath, swear an oath, oath of loyalty.
exercise: «The witness stood in court and spoke under oath.» What does under oath mean here? | Standing very still | Sleeping in the courtroom | *Having promised in court to tell the truth | Shouting angrily at the judge
exercise: «The knight swore an oath of loyalty to his king.» What is an oath in this sentence? | A loud shout of anger | A type of horse | *A serious promise of loyalty | A map of the castle
exercise: «He let out an oath when he stubbed his toe.» In this old-fashioned sentence, what does oath mean? | A quiet song | A promise to tell the truth | A kind wish for good luck | *A swear word said in anger
test: «Mia took an oath to tell the truth.» What does take an oath mean? | Take a long nap | *Make a serious promise | Forget the truth | Buy a new coat
test: Which phrase means having promised in a court to tell the truth? | Out of breath | Over the moon | *Under oath | In a hurry
exam: Which sentence uses oath as a serious promise, not a swear word? | «He let out an oath when the dog barked.» | «He shouted an oath at the wet car.» | *«The new president took an oath to defend the country.» | «The oath was loud and rude.»
